WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.570
- So welcome everyone,

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:13.390
to the Fall 2021 Refugees Seminar Series.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:17.700
Today we have the pleasure
of having Professor Nugent

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.450
talking about the activism from exile

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.450
and how activists abroad
influence politics back home.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.230
Professor Nugent is an Assistant Professor

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.150
of Political Science at Yale University.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.840
She is the author of After Repression,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.490
How Polarization Derails
Democratic Transition,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.800
published by Princeton
University Press in 2020.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.960
Her research explored
the political psychology

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.600
of religion and repression
in the Middle East,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.890
and has been published or is forthcoming

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.650
in the American Journal
of Political Science,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.280
Comparative political studies,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.090
Journal of Conflict Resolution Proceedings

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:05.090
of the National Academy of
Science and/or politics.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.660
Professor Nugent holds
a Doctorate in Politics

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.510
from Princeton University,

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.970
as well as a Bachelor in Arts in Arabic,

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.980
and Master in Arts in Arabic studies,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.270
both from Georgetown University.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.920
She previously was a
postdoctoral research fellow

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.162
in the Middle East Initiative

00:01:24.162 --> 00:01:26.630
at Harvard Kennedy School of Government,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.393
Belfer Center for Science
and International Affairs.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.540
So this is our hybrid seminar,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.540
we have attendees both in
the Luce Center and in so.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.923
So Professor Nugent 
the floor is yours, thanks.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.400
- Great, well, thank you so
much for joining in Lewis.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.170
And to everyone else who's joining online,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.183
I really appreciate the program.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:55.790
Over the last two years

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.120
that I've been working on this project,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.950
it's obviously been quite
interrupted by the pandemic.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.680
And the small grant that
I have from the program

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.250
allowed me to basically shift
a lot of what I was doing

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.817
to online research and online interviews

00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:12.617
so I'll talk a little bit
about during the presentation.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.590
So the broader project that
I'm gonna talk about today,

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.590
takes on start the Arab Spring
uprisings since 2011 to 2012.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.980
This is a picture from Tahrir Square,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.943
which was the epicenter of
the revolution in Egypt.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.700
In my first book, I talked
a lot about how essentially

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.160
the revolution went wrong, right.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.220
So how it resulted in
authoritarian retrenchment

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.111
within two years.

00:02:43.111 --> 00:02:45.950
But one of the first quotes
that I cite in the book

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.830
is from somebody who was in Tampere,

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.130
for those 18 days of protests.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.100
He described it as being like a dream.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.040
So it felt like everything was possible,

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.340
there was a sense of camaraderie

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.453
with strangers and countrymen.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.470
One author describes it
as liminality, right,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.830
that everything that felt
like it had been established

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.160
was now up for question in really positive

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.950
and interesting ways.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:10.250
And for a brief moment,
in the Egyptian case,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.640
there was actually civilian government.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.670
So from 2012 to 2013.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.310
Chris was a Muslim Brotherhood affiliate,

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.450
but there was someone

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.653
who was not a military figure in charge.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.830
The thing that within two years,

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.990
the revolution is essentially over.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.463
What I'm gonna call it and I'll define

00:03:27.463 --> 00:03:30.353
this in a couple of slides
is a defeated revolution.

00:03:30.353 --> 00:03:33.030
This gentleman here Abdel Fattah al-Sisi

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.380
the current president of Egypt.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.840
In July 2013, he announced
on national television

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.570
that the military would
be performing a coup,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.493
removing the democratically
elected president from office.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.460
He then won reelection twice

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.920
with kind of the standard
94 97% of the vote

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.610
and a pretty regular
authoritarian election.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.530
Since then he's overseen
retrenchment of authoritarianism

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.860
and that's much worse than
what his predecessors oversaw.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.440
So in the first year after the coup,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.970
there were 40,000 people
that were arrested,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.400
most affiliated with
the Muslim Brotherhood.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.470
Since then, that number
has grown to 100,000

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.870
probably 60,000 of those
are political prisoners

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.830
and beyond Sisi has recently reached

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.330
his constitutional term limit

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.010
and so he pushed the new amendments

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.940
not only allowed him to
stay in power through 2030,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.313
but also institutionalizing
military rule in the country.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.520
So Egypt even though, it's
obviously a very heartbreaking

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.390
and sad story, it's also
not an anomalous story.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.990
So most revolutions fail, right

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.678
two out of three since 1900 to 2014

00:04:59.678 --> 00:05:03.610
there have been 345
revolutionary episodes.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:04.890
Two thirds of those have failed.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.570
Only 123 have actually resulted
in meaningful political,

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.343
economic or social change.

00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:14.130
And what's interesting is
that despite its prevalence,

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.310
this is the modal outcome,

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.620
we know very little about
what happens afterwards.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.310
We know a lot about
successful revolutions.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.290
The literature tends to focus
on these are interesting,

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:28.083
and they're particularly
unique in certain ways.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.540
So what I'm gonna talk about today

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:36.000
is a new book project
and a related paper on

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.220
taking efforts inspiration
because of what has happened

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.020
in Egypt over the last few years.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.760
So I am particularly
interested in how activists

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.256
experience defeat of revolution,

00:05:46.256 --> 00:05:49.200
and then how it affects their
subsequent political behavior.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.550
I'm gonna start by giving you an overview

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.340
of my book tentatively titled

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.423
Exiles: How Activists Brought
Influence Politics Back Home.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.640
What I'm gonna do in this
is kind of conceptualize

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.880
what defeated revolution is for activists,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.370
these are people who are acutely
aware of what's going on.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.380
They're also involved with both planning

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.723
and executing revolutions.

00:06:10.597 --> 00:06:14.490
What I'm gonna argue is that
combination of experiences

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.930
and the context that they end up in,

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.420
as you'll see in the case of Egypt,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.433
most activists who are not
in jail are now in exile,

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.020
because that's a quite a profound impact

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.880
on whether they continue
to be active as activists

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.041
and the nature of that activism.

00:06:30.041 --> 00:06:31.480
And as I mentioned, I'm gonna focus

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.840
mostly on Egyptian exiles,
so Syrian, Iraqi and Libyan

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.073
exiles come up as an
interesting comparison.

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:41.840
Then I'm gonna talk about a related

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.370
but standalone coauthor paper

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.410
with Alex Siegel at the
University of Colorado Boulder.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.220
She really is the expert

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:51.360
for dealing with Arabic
language, digital data.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.170
What we do here is look at
a specific case of protests

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:56.710
in Egypt in September 2019,

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.790
where exiles were extremely influential

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:02.370
in introducing and then
amplifying information

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.883
that then resulted in
pretty significant protests.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.667
So what comes after defeated revolution?

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.917
This is kind of a big
question that I'm interested

00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:19.000
in this book.

00:07:19.914 --> 00:07:22.740
I have a quote up here that
hopefully you can see online

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.250
as well I'll just read it.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:27.470
This from Sarah Hegazi the
late Egyptian activists,

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.270
she said, "As Saint-Just
said, "those who make half

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.330
a revolution dig their own graves.'

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.350
It was literally, not
just figuratively true.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:38.410
Most of us are now in the
grave, in prison or in exile."

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:43.270
Sarah was a communist
activist who died by suicide

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.020
following a pretty terrible ordeal.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:51.020
She waved an LGBTQ pride flag
at a (indistinct) concert

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:55.710
in Cairo resulted in her
being arrested, tortured,

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.010
the PTS said that she
suffered from was pretty huge

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.663
she ended up in exile in Osho Canada.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.293
So what is the experience
like for activists?

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:12.040
There are some obvious ways
that we can think about this.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.440
First, there's a decrease
in the quality of democracy

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:15.610
in Egypt, right?

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.630
There was this one year civilian rule.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.300
There were two years of elections,

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.490
there were two years of liberalization.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.240
So activists were saying that's a decrease

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.760
in that quality specifically
and about politics, right?

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.833
If you remember that first
quote I just showed you.

00:08:30.833 --> 00:08:32.670
At the beginning, it felt like a dream,

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:34.130
it felt like everything was possible

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:36.300
life was going to change significantly.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.630
But politically, socially, economically,

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:40.570
personally, a number of people

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:41.900
that I talked to, for this project

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.400
have had major falling outs
with family and friends

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.660
in ways that also toxic
to the personal aspects

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:52.323
of defeated revolution.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.330
In addition, especially
in the case of Egypt,

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.960
the instability that the
revolution introduced

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.140
among its defeat, exacerbated,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.790
these folks are gonna be
exposed to economic decline.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:06.030
But most importantly,

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.333
and this is why I told
you Sarahs backstory,

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:11.730
and a moment of defeat of revolution,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.070
the state is going to be quite defensive.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.420
They're reestablishing themselves.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:18.950
What happened is that activists

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.470
are those that are targeted most acutely

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:21.820
by state repression.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:23.470
As I mentioned, 100,000
have been arrested,

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.350
60,000 of those are political prisoners.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.670
Countless have been
disappeared into the system,

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.860
their lawyers and their
families can't find them.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:32.980
What I'm gonna focus on here

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:35.108
is this group of individuals
who have been exiled

00:09:35.108 --> 00:09:38.008
so there's potentially 10s of
1000s, who have been exiled.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.820
This is a form of course migration

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.743
that functions in a way that's
very similar to regression.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.540
So before I get too much further,

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:49.860
let me define a couple of terms

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.150
just so we're all on the same page here.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.660
So first, the way that
I think of revolution

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.490
is a massive of established government,

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.290
involving competing claims to sovereignty,

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:03.060
which aims to displace
the incumbent regime

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.350
and substantially alter
the established order.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.590
Now, scholars will often differentiate

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.030
between political revolutions

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.320
right where the established order

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:14.080
that's being changed as political.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.340
My interlocutors really
think of political,

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.250
social and economic
changes going together.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:21.473
So the established order here
is to the society at large.

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:26.080
What I mean by defeated revolution

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.990
is when this process
doesn't happen, right,

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:31.980
so the competing senators
of sovereignty emerge,

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:34.370
the way Tilly talks about this,

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.020
there's a revolutionary situation,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:37.140
there's a potential for change,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.290
and it doesn't result
in the final instance,

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.263
and meaningful change to
the established order.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.510
I think it's a bit differently
than other scholars

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.380
in political science do.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.380
Often there's a focus on
the sequence or process

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.460
through which revolutions emerge,

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.700
or the structural
predecessors that determine

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:01.080
whether or not a revolution
is successful eventually.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:04.030
But I'm thinking about this
as a participatory experience.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:06.910
So something that happens to activists

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.860
not necessarily something that they do.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:17.460
Second, activists, this is a definition

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.350
I take from (inaudible) an individual

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:22.070
with long-term involvements in the form

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.050
of collective protest action,

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.070
in the context of movements,
groups, or campaigns

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.320
that were structured to
at least some extent.

00:11:29.559 --> 00:11:31.250
Two parts that I wanna highlight here.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.510
So first, long-term involvements
isn't an important thing,

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:37.090
activists or people who think of activism

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.810
as a political behavior that they use

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.830
not just in the revolutionary situation

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:43.240
that I just referred to

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.330
but as a way of contesting
politics more broadly.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:48.120
In the case of Egypt, this means

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.760
that they're not just active in 2011.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.533
They're active before 2011,
and they're active since.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.590
Second, this definition
speaks to the collective.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.700
So individual protests is very interesting

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.480
but it's hard to generalize,
it's hard to observe.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.060
Here being involved in movements, groups,

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.940
unions, organizations, things
that are collective entities

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.030
is really important for
thinking about somebody

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.553
as being an activist.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.100
And then finally, exile is
just one of the key terms

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:21.290
in the book project.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:24.280
So as I mentioned, that
results from course migration

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.540
regimes use this as a
political punishment,

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.410
for political behavior.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.280
It's very similar to other logics

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.470
in terms of why regimes use it.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.970
So the idea here is to
demobilize individuals,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:41.970
fracture the opposition, make
anti-regime mobilization,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.633
unsuccessful and ineffective.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:47.720
One thing that became very
obvious in my research

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:51.100
is that this is a difficult
thing to sometimes pin down.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.460
One thing that happens to people,

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.090
or one kind of migration
status that exiles hold.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:58.960
So the way I talk about this in the book

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.750
is that there are both explicit
and formal forms of exile,

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.610
and then implicit and
informal forms of exile.

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.650
So let's start with
explicit informal here,

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:10.840
the focus is going to be on
revocation of citizenship,

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:13.490
or travel ban, with informal,

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:16.900
it's much more the regime
shows up at your house

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.780
in the form of the police,
makes it very evident

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.800
that you're no longer a protected citizen,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.913
less formal, but still
just as potentially scary.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.180
Additional thing that I've
found in some of my research,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.680
so often, exiles will also
talk about the decline

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:38.230
in the quality of politics
and economics in Egypt

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:39.840
as a reason for why they left.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:40.690
So I'm trying to think

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.690
about how that might also
be regime strategies,

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:48.690
or a tactic of wanting people to leave.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.203
So where have Egypt's
activist gone since 2011?

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.640
Both of these graphs on
the next one are inflows,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:02.763
and then asylum claims to OECD countries.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.640
As you can see, 2011 is
gonna be this dotted line.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:08.400
This is the year of the revolution.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:09.870
2013 is about here.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.640
So these are major events.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.790
First, the revolution
and the (indistinct).

00:14:16.231 --> 00:14:18.180
In the decade is prior to the revolution,

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:23.180
there are about 239,000 Egyptian
migrants to OECD countries.

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:28.143
That number grows to 339,000 after.

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:32.700
We can see an even
bigger spike here, right.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:36.870
So before the revolution,
about 13,000 Egyptians

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.830
had applied for asylum in OECD countries,

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:41.500
but 10 years after the revolution,

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.463
116,000 had applied for asylum.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:50.450
So significant numbers,
particularly after 2011.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:51.523
That's kind of interesting, right?

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:54.440
I mean, in a case like
Syria, the revolutions

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:59.440
led to a civil war, the
security context have declined.

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:00.997
We might expect a graph like this.

00:15:00.997 --> 00:15:03.930
And in fact the numbers are quite large,

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:05.611
but Egypt hasn't become any safe,

00:15:05.611 --> 00:15:09.060
any less safe in terms
of national security

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:09.893
in the last few years.

00:15:09.893 --> 00:15:12.660
So there's clearly something
politically motivated going on.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:18.070
From my quality of evidence,

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:19.977
and my interviews with activists,

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:22.980
one thing that I've uncovered

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.500
is that the demographics of migrants

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.160
have changed over the last 10 years.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:31.330
So the Brotherhood, its
activists and supporters,

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:33.880
its leaders, those who weren't arrested,

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.610
left 2013, essentially, as the coup

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:37.537
is happening (indistinct).

00:15:38.707 --> 00:15:43.707
And in 2014, the CC
regime having more or less

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:45.500
dealt with the Brotherhood and involves

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:48.040
legal and repressive waves starts to look

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.440
at the rest of the active opposition

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:52.895
and starts to target
Non-Islamic activists.

00:15:52.895 --> 00:15:56.460
And they start to leave in
larger numbers starting in 2014.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.343
And then again, in 2018,
right around the elections.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.570
What's been interesting is watching

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:09.900
the self of activists popping
up in different locations

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:11.063
over the last 10 years.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:15.530
So there are certain Western
destinations in Germany

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:18.610
and we have Berlin, in the
US, there's New York City

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:20.780
and Washington DC.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.120
There's also places closer to the region,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.583
including Doha and Instabul.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.940
Interestingly, there are three interesting

00:16:31.859 --> 00:16:34.860
places of exiles of Italy, Milan, Korea,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:36.823
and Seoul and Lebanon, Beirut.

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:40.420
This has to do with the
ease of which victims

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:42.040
can travel to these places.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.703
They don't need visas to
go into these countries,

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:50.230
particularly in Seoul,
this was an early hub.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:52.670
And since the migration restrictions

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:54.270
have actually been put down,

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.570
so there's not as many
people migrating there.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:03.160
So the broader question
I'm asking in this project

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:07.510
is what's been interesting
is watching how activism

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:11.450
does or doesn't change once
people left the country, right.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:13.460
So first, what influences
whether activists

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.970
continue to be activists in exile.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:18.910
And second, what influences
that form, right.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:20.460
What they're doing as activism.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:26.420
As a first choice, so
here we have activism,

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:29.390
we have our activists who
are then being exiled.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.650
In the first order, they
have three main choices.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:36.440
They cease to be activists
as individual demobilization,

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:39.550
they leave the movement
they leave activism,

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:41.390
do they convert?

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.920
This is the term that I use here.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:45.240
So they start to mobilize on other issues.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:48.430
Concrete example of this
might be Egyptian activists

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:52.850
in exile in Berlin had to start
to work on migration issues

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:54.370
in Germany, or the Green Movement.

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:57.753
So we no longer focus on
domestic issues in Egypt.

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:03.080
If they continue to mobilize on Egyptian

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.090
based domestic issues,

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:07.860
they have three main behavioral choices.

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:10.370
Do they work as an activist
to prepare the movement

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:12.400
for regime change down the road?

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.210
Or do they take a combative approach

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:17.410
either promoting bottom up regime change

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:20.010
through support networks
back home on the ground,

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:23.560
or promoting top down
regime change by lobbying

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.603
their new hosts in their new countries?

00:18:27.983 --> 00:18:30.343
The theoris in how they
make these decisions,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:32.660
I think both combination of individual

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:35.240
and contextual factors come into play,

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:38.240
but at different times
during the decision process.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:39.770
So in terms of individual factors,

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:40.900
I think this is really important

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:42.580
for that first decision, right?

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:45.190
Whether or not you continue in activism,

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:49.280
convert to activism, or
cease to be an activist.

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:50.780
Here looking at some of the literature

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:54.910
on the biographical effects of activism,

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:57.330
we see that the length
of activism matters.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:01.060
So people who joined activism before 2011

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:03.150
tend to be much more committed

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:04.970
in ways that suggests
they're going to continue

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.770
even after they leave their country.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.510
The existence of personal
networks back home.

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:13.380
Can the regime literally
repress your family,

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.330
even though you're no longer part

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.123
of kind of the domestic sphere.

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.390
Experiences of repression
can go one of two ways.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:24.240
So some people who experienced
repression and torture

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.603
as a result of the
defeat of the revolution,

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.650
they're galvanized, they're angry,

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:32.120
the parts of their identity

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:35.170
for which they were repressed are stronger

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:36.790
and more mobilizing.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.780
For other people they have real trauma,

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:42.433
real PTSD in ways that prevent
them from being an activist.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:47.670
Then finally, I consider
two demographic conditions,

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.840
the first class and education.

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:51.630
So do you have the literal resources

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.400
that you need to continue
to be an activist,

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:56.300
particularly when it's very difficult?

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.010
And secondly, are you in a phase of life

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.040
where you may be working on other things,

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:03.950
like education or even having children.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:06.960
This in the American literature
is particularly important

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.663
for why people individually
didn't rise from activism.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:15.590
In terms of the contextual factors,

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:18.200
I'll look at three things,
the first regime type,

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:21.040
this is democracy where
people have the ability

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:23.920
to lobby their representatives,

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:28.800
have their preferences
accounted for in policies,

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:31.680
what are the relationships like
between the new host country

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.460
and the home country?

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.370
You'll see them in my cases
there's a very big difference

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.890
between the United
States, Germany and Turkey

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.103
in terms of the relationship with Egypt.

00:20:43.402 --> 00:20:45.160
And then finally thinking about migration,

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:49.390
the extent to which migrants
have essentially assimilated

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.550
which has to do with
the number of migrants

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:54.200
how they're treated, whether
or not there are other

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.400
similar migrants who are
treated similarly to them.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.730
In this case, Arab and Muslim migrants,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.450
matters for whether or not
they're going to keep mobilizing

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.610
on domestic issues back home,

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:07.223
or issues related to the new host country.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.500
So putting this all together,

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.100
this is the data that I'm working with,

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:17.000
it's a mixed activism project.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:18.970
Much of this has been delayed by COVID.

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:21.503
But I'm hopeful that it's
gonna get done in 2022.

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:23.820
As I mentioned, the program's funding

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:27.900
allowed me to complete a data
set of Egyptian migration,

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.370
a data set of travel
bans and arrests in Egypt

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:31.990
under the CC regime,

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:33.400
and also to keep translating

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:36.210
and transcribing my semi
structured interviews,

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:37.344
which I was able to complete

00:21:37.344 --> 00:21:39.883
(indistinct) the last 18 months.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.870
So just a couple of notes on
some preliminary findings.

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:49.460
These two gentlemen
were both administrating

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:54.370
the (indistinct) Facebook pages
was an extremely important

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:58.260
Facebook page that organized
the protests in 2011,

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.260
from the top we have Abdullah al-Nadeem

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:02.950
on the bottom, we have Abdulla Rasha.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:05.740
both of them have
experienced course migration,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:08.330
they were both arrested, tortured.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.580
Both of them have experienced
having family members

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:12.990
questioned by the regimes.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.600
They reacted in very different ways.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:18.550
So right there on the top, he
now lives in San Francisco,

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:21.190
he's really no longer what
we might call an activist.

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:24.201
He does do some individual mobilization,

00:22:24.201 --> 00:22:26.250
but he's disconnected from
the networks of people

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.600
that he worked with before,

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:32.220
set a couple of very public sad meltdowns,

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:33.700
I guess he would call them

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:37.100
and he now works in the tech industry.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.440
Abdullah al-Nadeem remains very networked,

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.530
he's now living in New York City,

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:44.710
continues to very quietly mobilize

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:49.710
among both the German and the
American networks of exiles.

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:55.730
Let me speak a little bit about
the importance of context.

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:58.760
In the United States,
there's been an interesting

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.440
development of the Egypt
Human Rights Caucus.

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.590
This is led by in Congress,

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:08.040
it's Don Beyer and Tom Malinowski,

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.780
I believe in Virginia and New Jersey.

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:15.009
Here, activists have been
working on forming this caucus

00:23:15.009 --> 00:23:17.457
to talk about things like human rights

00:23:17.457 --> 00:23:20.010
and military aid to Egypt.

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:21.020
So it's very specific, right?

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:23.297
We're not talking about
regime change here.

00:23:23.297 --> 00:23:26.180
And that has very much
to do with the nature

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:29.640
of the relationship between
Egypt and the United States.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.040
It's also shaped by the
political opportunity structure.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.150
So here before the caucus,
they're lobbying the government

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:37.750
because that's how politics
works the United States.

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:43.320
In the case of Germany, you
see a much more diffuse,

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:46.760
less organized, less
hierarchical structure.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:48.810
There tends to be a lot
of cross pollination,

00:23:48.810 --> 00:23:52.686
Berlin has a huge Syrian exile population.

00:23:52.686 --> 00:23:56.170
And so you see Egyptians working
in kind of pan Arab issues.

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:59.460
This is a protest that Egyptians
worked with Palestinians

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:02.010
to mobilize these issues.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.520
There's some mobilization
in terms of lobbying

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.850
the government or testifying
in front of the government

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:11.180
on certain issues but it
can be much more diffused,

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:12.013
much more focused

00:24:12.013 --> 00:24:15.573
on what we would call
traditional activism.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:23.960
So now, I wanna get

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:26.650
into the coauthored paper with Alexival.

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:27.860
In this case, we're gonna be looking

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:32.020
at specifically protests in Egypt in 2019.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.950
And how exiles participated in introducing

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:38.190
and mobilizing these protests.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:40.100
The broader question we're asking

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:43.090
is how do exiles participate
in contentious politics

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:45.260
back home in the digital age?

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:48.830
And to theorize this we draw
on three sets of literature

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.110
and some of their essential concepts.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.040
So first, we think of the internet

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:58.120
as extending the domestic sphere abroad.

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:02.050
So this is a place in which
there's a transnational

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:03.913
sense of political identity,

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:06.500
exiles who're no longer physically

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:08.627
located within the country

00:25:08.627 --> 00:25:11.380
can to continue to be in
contact and even deepen

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.760
certain kinds of connections
with people back home.

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:19.430
And it essentially allows
political exiles to have agency

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:20.860
to have some kind of autonomy

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:22.893
in influencing domestic politics.

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:29.630
Second, we rely on the political models

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:32.540
of how we think about people
and how they've joined

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:34.980
activists protests mobilization,

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:37.450
to think about what the internet
might be doing similarly

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:40.000
to these classic models.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.550
So we know the individual
models of protests,

00:25:42.550 --> 00:25:44.580
mobilization, that information provision

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:46.350
is extremely important, right.

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:48.340
So learning that lots of other people

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.083
support oppositional position

00:25:50.083 --> 00:25:53.283
makes it less costly for
the individual to join.

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:55.523
What we're thinking

00:25:55.523 --> 00:25:57.590
about here is digital
information provision.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:02.230
And here, this, again, speaks
to the the classic models,

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:03.903
but we're talking about essentially

00:26:03.903 --> 00:26:06.160
when something goes viral online.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:09.320
So when things are posted
by multiple people,

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:12.223
in a way that suggests that
lots of people support it.

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:17.940
This is important for
authoritarian regimes

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:21.130
in a way that may be less
important for democratic issues.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:23.930
So online, it's slightly more free,

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:26.850
at least in the context of Egypt,

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.830
it may be the kind of place where engaging

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:30.250
with oppositional information

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.610
here meeting critical of the regime

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.220
signal something read about how popular

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:36.683
an oppositional position is.

00:26:39.140 --> 00:26:41.840
And then finally, we think that exiles

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:44.810
might actually have quite
a unique role to play

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:46.430
in digital information cascade.

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:50.250
So as you saw exiles
obviously are all located

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:51.792
outside of the country.

00:26:51.792 --> 00:26:53.620
None of them are located
in democratic contexts

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:56.760
where they have relatively more impunity,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.120
to be posting oppositional information,

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.950
and doing so over time
and connects us in a way

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:05.410
that had they been in the country,

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:07.760
they might have been
repressed or (indistinct).

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.730
To think about how this
whole process works,

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:17.120
we draw by the communication
literature talk

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.740
about three different kinds
of social media users.

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.810
So first influencers these are the users

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:27.400
that see new content, they
make memes, they make hashtags.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.080
This case, they also make videos,

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:32.663
some of the literature
first time as idea starters.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:36.630
There's amplifiers, these
are, as the literature

00:27:36.630 --> 00:27:38.210
calls them firehose of knowledge,

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:42.190
they're doing all for lack of
a better word amplification,

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:43.023
of this information.

00:27:43.023 --> 00:27:45.633
So taking the content, making it go viral.

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:49.280
These are extremely important users,

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:51.360
because they're often very active.

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:54.580
They'll do things like in the
case under consideration here.

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:56.780
They're going to retreat at a high rate.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:58.670
They're going to add
people to conversations

00:27:58.670 --> 00:27:59.870
and then connect people.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:02.730
Then finally we have consumers.

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:04.650
In our theory these are also gonna end up

