schools and potholes

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:12.303
and broken streetlights.

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:14.900
What we do is scrape it to see

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:18.390
if they're talking about any of this

00:42:18.390 --> 00:42:19.993
right at a very local level.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.030
And we find that they're talking

00:42:23.030 --> 00:42:26.420
about the oppositional content
in very different ways.

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:30.300
So this, the blue here
is going to be places

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:31.563
where protests occurred.

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:37.010
The pink is going to be places
where they did not occur.

00:42:37.010 --> 00:42:40.670
What we see here is that
places where protests occurred,

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:43.550
talked about Mohamed Ali's
oppositional content,

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:47.853
posted it, discussed it, prior
to the start of protests.

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:50.400
And then they stopped talking about it.

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:52.610
And we think there's some
kind of repressive backlash

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:54.110
there where they're no longer willing

00:42:54.110 --> 00:42:56.933
to talk about something
that is potentially costly.

00:42:57.860 --> 00:42:59.270
In the non-protest areas,

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:01.800
they only talk about that
content after protests.

00:43:01.800 --> 00:43:03.130
So they're learning something

00:43:03.130 --> 00:43:05.600
about this oppositional contract

00:43:05.600 --> 00:43:10.600
from likely that nationwide
news coverage of these events.

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:14.430
So we think of this the
quality of literature

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:16.230
is much more of a smoking gun.

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:20.510
Just there is something about
local information consumption

00:43:20.510 --> 00:43:21.833
of this oppositional information

00:43:21.833 --> 00:43:24.300
that matters for whether
or not you see protests

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:25.133
or whether or not you're learning

00:43:25.133 --> 00:43:26.460
about that information later.

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:34.280
So just to recap

00:43:35.260 --> 00:43:37.420
here exiles in our story
are really important

00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:39.810
for introducing and
amplifying information.

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:41.570
They contribute to information cascades,

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:44.760
which are so important for
getting people to protest,

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.150
but they don't lead interest rate.

00:43:46.150 --> 00:43:47.900
You'll remember in all of the graphs,

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:50.520
there was more online
engagement from domestic users

00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:52.273
than exile users.

00:43:53.210 --> 00:43:56.710
Finally, we show that there's
a very fine grained level

00:43:56.710 --> 00:43:58.670
of correlation between local protests

00:43:58.670 --> 00:44:02.743
and local consumption of this
oppositional information.

00:44:04.790 --> 00:44:06.140
As I mentioned, there may be two things

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:08.270
that are somewhat specific to this case,

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:10.420
although we're only looking at Egypt,

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:12.950
it would certainly be
worth thinking about Egypt

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:14.480
in comparative perspective.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:17.380
The influencer is not only credible

00:44:17.380 --> 00:44:19.380
as the kid say, has received from having

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:24.380
been personally involved in
being an army contractor,

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:28.180
he's not partisan.

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:30.440
In a place like Egypt being identified

00:44:30.440 --> 00:44:32.760
as either an Islamist or non Islamist,

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:36.307
in meaningful ways might
have made his information,

00:44:36.307 --> 00:44:38.740
less credible to lots of people.

00:44:38.740 --> 00:44:41.900
On the other side, he emphasizes
throughout his videos,

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:43.530
his blue colored background,

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:46.413
which is kind of an every
man in the situation.

00:44:47.530 --> 00:44:49.450
As I mentioned, he also
worked as a celebrity.

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:52.170
So he's very polished,
he's very charismatic,

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:56.670
his videos are shot in
like a selfie style,

00:44:56.670 --> 00:44:58.543
but they're very polished.

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:03.530
Second, because he works in
the entertainment industry,

00:45:03.530 --> 00:45:05.950
he seems to have a lot of followers

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:08.830
who are in the media and
the entertainment sectors.

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:11.323
And in the literature
in the United States,

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:14.810
people tend to be bored of politics,

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:16.280
they don't like politics.

00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:18.620
Celebrities can get people
excited about politics.

00:45:18.620 --> 00:45:20.410
And so he has a high
number of these people

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:22.410
who are considered to be icons

00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:23.750
in the communications literature

00:45:23.750 --> 00:45:27.073
that are retweeting
especially Ali in his work.

00:45:29.810 --> 00:45:34.800
So to bring it back to a higher level,

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:36.040
what I'm hoping to do here

00:45:36.040 --> 00:45:37.690
is think about the micro foundations

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:40.640
of both revolution and
defeated revolution,

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:43.140
as well as democratization.

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:46.900
So quite a few of the activists
that I am talking about,

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:49.090
in both the paper that
I'm working on with Alex

00:45:49.090 --> 00:45:53.190
and in the book, these are people

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:56.060
who not only planned and
executed the revolution,

00:45:56.060 --> 00:45:59.653
but they continued it to be
completed continued beyond 2011.

00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:02.290
And the political science literature

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:05.290
has talked a lot about how
their biographical effects

00:46:05.290 --> 00:46:08.830
of having participated in movements.

00:46:08.830 --> 00:46:10.900
Here, I'm interested in this concept

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:15.159
of how participating in a revolution,

00:46:15.159 --> 00:46:18.730
and then a defeated revolution
affect the rest of your life.

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:21.540
For many new people that
happen in their early 20s.

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:24.620
They solve a long time to
grapple with the effects

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:27.070
and it's completely under
studied and literature.

00:46:29.483 --> 00:46:32.080
Second, I'm interested in kind of doing

00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:33.840
this and other projects as well

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:36.040
with one of our colleagues
here, Dan Natali.

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:43.150
Course migration is a very
interesting form of migration.

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:45.260
And we spend a lot of
time in political science

00:46:45.260 --> 00:46:47.900
thinking about transnational
repression, right?

00:46:47.900 --> 00:46:51.780
What regimes are doing to punish activists

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:53.650
beyond their borders.

00:46:53.650 --> 00:46:56.270
But we think less about
how this is inadvertently

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:57.960
creating transnational opposition.

00:46:57.960 --> 00:46:59.980
And so we wanna think a
little bit more critically

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:02.610
about that how the way in which regimes

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:05.670
use course migration, if
they use course migration,

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:09.360
shapes opposition in ways
that has important effects

00:47:09.360 --> 00:47:10.773
on domestic politics.

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:15.700
And finally, perhaps, hopefully,

00:47:15.700 --> 00:47:19.343
a lot of this has not
been particularly helpful.

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:24.120
The folks that are currently
located outside of Egypt

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:26.430
who are doing things like introducing

00:47:26.430 --> 00:47:30.340
oppositional information
and mobilizing protests

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:34.100
will likely be a source of
democratization in the future.

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:35.940
Most of the Arab Spring uprisings

00:47:35.940 --> 00:47:40.250
did not result in a
meaningful democratization,

00:47:40.250 --> 00:47:44.290
they very much resulted in
increased authoritarianism.

00:47:44.290 --> 00:47:47.000
As a result, most of the
open space for contestation

00:47:47.000 --> 00:47:48.550
is outside of the country.

00:47:48.550 --> 00:47:52.410
So just like 2010, to 2011,
the Arab Spring uprisings

00:47:53.450 --> 00:47:57.243
rely on decades of people
working as activists,

00:47:57.243 --> 00:48:00.029
I think, any kind of
future democratization too.

00:48:00.029 --> 00:48:01.560
And in the case of the Arab Spring,

00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:05.120
right where we are now
with regards to oppression,

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:06.730
likely those activists
are gonna be the ones

00:48:06.730 --> 00:48:08.973
located outside of the country.

00:48:10.100 --> 00:48:12.280
So with that, I will say thank you,

00:48:12.280 --> 00:48:15.220
thank you again, to the
program for helping me

00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:17.470
to continue this research
over the last year.

00:48:19.722 --> 00:48:23.538
- You're Welcome.

00:48:23.538 --> 00:48:24.679
- So what do I do now?

00:48:24.679 --> 00:48:25.861
(laughing)

00:48:25.861 --> 00:48:30.861
- (indistinct) the
people from the audience

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:34.883
would like to ask questions now.

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:39.350
Pass them to Professor Nugent.

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:50.673
- Yeah, true.

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:53.940
- [Man] So I have two questions.

00:48:53.940 --> 00:48:58.940
So the first is sort of a
sugary governing framing

00:49:02.980 --> 00:49:07.980
or the types of cases that
your analysis applies to.

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:14.020
And I was wondering whether
this just applies to exile

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:16.620
in the context of defeated revolution,

00:49:16.620 --> 00:49:21.420
or if it's broadly
applicable to any setting

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:26.020
where perhaps there's history
democratic backsliding.

00:49:26.020 --> 00:49:29.770
So I was thinking particularly
in the case of Turkey,

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:32.840
but then also in settings
where one side is defeated

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:37.526
in a civil war, and individuals
are forced to go abroad,

00:49:37.526 --> 00:49:41.260
but still there some attempt to try

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:43.420
and influence politics back home.

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:47.480
So like, I was wondering, the extent

00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:51.649
to which you think your argument applies

00:49:51.649 --> 00:49:55.680
to those settings that like
revolutionary settings,

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:58.140
in particular, whether
it can apply to a broader

00:49:58.140 --> 00:49:59.113
set of settings?

00:49:59.950 --> 00:50:02.753
And then the second
question I have is regarding

00:50:02.753 --> 00:50:05.920
the diagram that you had
where individuals abroad

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:10.920
have these choices to either remain active

00:50:11.200 --> 00:50:14.963
in politics back home as exiles,

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:19.160
or just change to some
other political activism

00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:21.176
or just disengage altogether.

00:50:21.176 --> 00:50:23.526
I was wondering the extent
to which individuals

00:50:25.256 --> 00:50:28.493
shift between those modes of behavior.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:33.390
But overall, really fascinating project.

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:35.503
- Should I answer it?

00:50:35.503 --> 00:50:38.503
okay, yeah, so your first question

00:50:38.503 --> 00:50:39.797
is a really interesting one,

00:50:39.797 --> 00:50:42.450
because as I mentioned at
the beginning of the talk

00:50:42.450 --> 00:50:46.080
of the 345 revolutionary situations

00:50:46.080 --> 00:50:49.880
that don't, so 123 of them
are successful, right,

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:53.420
meaning they result in
democratization in a meaningful way.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:57.340
The rest of them fail in
lots of different ways.

00:50:57.340 --> 00:50:59.410
So some become civil wars,

00:50:59.410 --> 00:51:01.898
some just go back to the
same authoritarianism.

00:51:01.898 --> 00:51:04.950
Some go back to worse authoritarianism.

00:51:04.950 --> 00:51:08.520
My case Kelly Clark has done
some really interesting work

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:11.130
on this concept of counter revolution.

00:51:11.130 --> 00:51:13.160
Egypt and I think he's right on this,

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:15.440
although I think the concept
of defeated revolution

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:17.763
can apply to a lot of different things.

00:51:19.060 --> 00:51:20.370
He calls it counter revolution,

00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:23.420
where you actually did
have a civilian government,

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:26.057
or a change in the Old
Guard in a meaningful way.

00:51:26.057 --> 00:51:29.480
And I think he says that there's 22 cases

00:51:29.480 --> 00:51:33.540
of that, where you have
meaningful change in government

00:51:33.540 --> 00:51:34.823
and then a reversal.

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:39.573
So I think, at the very least,
it applies to those 22 cases.

00:51:40.950 --> 00:51:43.600
Some of the other cases that
I'm looking at in the book,

00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:46.510
so Iraq, you end up with exiles before,

00:51:46.510 --> 00:51:48.110
like, basically, under Saddam Hussein,

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:51.153
that's not necessarily the
result of a failed revolution.

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:55.633
In Libya, you also part
of that, there are moments

00:51:55.633 --> 00:51:57.570
of civil war, there's also moments

00:51:57.570 --> 00:51:58.943
of just higher oppression.

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:01.953
In Syria, it's a defeat a revolution

00:52:01.953 --> 00:52:03.350
that has become a civil war.

00:52:03.350 --> 00:52:07.630
So it's not exactly, it's both right.

00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:11.010
That you can't separate that both

00:52:11.010 --> 00:52:12.550
of these things are related.

00:52:12.550 --> 00:52:14.130
So I'm trying to think
about defeated revolution

00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:17.020
as a broad category, but
then there would be lots

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:20.313
of different ways that
revolutions are failed or failing.

00:52:22.150 --> 00:52:23.830
Yeah, and in terms of that graph,

00:52:23.830 --> 00:52:28.570
I mean, I think there
probably is some switching.

00:52:28.570 --> 00:52:30.120
And at this point, it's been interesting

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:32.733
to see over the last 10 years,

00:52:32.733 --> 00:52:35.230
how people have evolved
in their stance, right?

00:52:35.230 --> 00:52:37.479
So people who have gone from
thinking that revolution

00:52:37.479 --> 00:52:38.520
is pointless that thinking,

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:41.200
Okay, well, now the
revolution must be global

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:44.140
instead of local, it
does seem like most folks

00:52:44.140 --> 00:52:46.920
who decide to cease being an activist

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:49.430
stay out of politics.

00:52:49.430 --> 00:52:51.000
That's not the case for
every single person,

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:53.390
but for the most part
they're making choices

00:52:53.390 --> 00:52:55.100
based on personal decisions,

00:52:55.100 --> 00:52:56.590
their children in or their family

00:52:56.590 --> 00:53:00.790
in needing to go back to
school, financial necessity,

00:53:00.790 --> 00:53:03.830
or they've experienced such
a terrible form of oppression

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:06.230
like they're unable to keep

00:53:06.230 --> 00:53:08.030
being active politically.

00:53:08.030 --> 00:53:12.620
There is more flip flopping or changing

00:53:12.620 --> 00:53:16.530
within people who continue to
be active on domestic issues,

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:18.450
and those who start to think about things

00:53:18.450 --> 00:53:20.890
as a broader issue.

00:53:20.890 --> 00:53:23.960
But it's pretty clear
when someone's saying,

00:53:23.960 --> 00:53:28.960
okay, I'm mobilizing the
cause of political detainees

00:53:30.620 --> 00:53:33.010
in Egypt versus political detainees.

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:35.901
And thinking about this in Egypt.

00:53:35.901 --> 00:53:38.640
The way in which they talk
about it does differentiate

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:39.473
between whether they're thinking

00:53:39.473 --> 00:53:41.140
about it as a broad category,

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:44.473
or if it's like in Egypt
specifically, this is the issue.

00:53:46.030 --> 00:53:47.630
So yeah, I think there's more overlap

00:53:47.630 --> 00:53:48.890
in those two categories.

00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:51.710
As people frankly, come to terms

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:53.380
with, are they gonna be staying

00:53:53.380 --> 00:53:55.470
in their new location for longer?

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:56.303
What else is happening in their life?

00:53:56.303 --> 00:53:59.760
Are there new focal points right around

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:02.050
which they could be
bringing the Egyptian issue

00:54:02.050 --> 00:54:06.170
into like the green movement
or into the migration movement?

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:09.587
Some of it's a bit strategic
in terms of that as well, yeah.

00:54:13.329 --> 00:54:17.750
- [Man] Thanks, so I had two
questions, one for each part.

00:54:17.750 --> 00:54:20.520
So for the first part of the talk,

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:24.453
I was curious what the city mentioned

00:54:24.453 --> 00:54:29.453
while and Abdulla taking
different paths in their activism,

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:31.170
I was wondering if you had a sense

00:54:31.170 --> 00:54:33.440
of why their paths diverged.

00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:34.860
And you mentioned trauma

00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:37.150
and I know they were
both exposed to trauma,

00:54:37.150 --> 00:54:39.410
but I'm not sure if you have any insight

00:54:39.410 --> 00:54:41.740
on why their paths might have taken

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:44.110
such different trajectories.

00:54:44.110 --> 00:54:47.330
And then the second question that I had

00:54:47.330 --> 00:54:50.807
is, you mentioned
transnational repression,

00:54:50.807 --> 00:54:55.807
and how Moh Ali was shut down eventually.

00:54:56.140 --> 00:55:00.942
And so I wonder, what if
you could talk a little bit

00:55:00.942 --> 00:55:05.942
about how exile activism
differs from activism at home,

00:55:09.140 --> 00:55:13.500
if you are still at the mercy
of transnational repression,

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:16.913
if your family members can
be targeted by the regime.

00:55:19.140 --> 00:55:21.900
Is it just like an
oversight that the regime

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:23.300
didn't notice these people,

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:25.580
but then once they pop
up, they get shut down?

00:55:25.580 --> 00:55:29.290
Or I feel like there there
could be some dynamic there.

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:32.000
Strategically, that would be
interesting to think about.

00:55:32.000 --> 00:55:33.840
- Yeah, these are good questions.

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:36.800
So in terms of the individual variation,

00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:40.120
I think in the case of
and Abdulla obviously,

00:55:40.120 --> 00:55:41.920
people are quite idiosyncratic,

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:43.940
but I think in this case, it had to do

00:55:43.940 --> 00:55:48.940
with their individual reactions
to trauma and repression.

00:55:49.460 --> 00:55:52.750
So they had very similar experiences.

00:55:52.750 --> 00:55:57.750
I think one of them as a
result had really terrible PTSD

00:55:59.636 --> 00:56:02.723
resulted in a crumbling
of his both his personal

00:56:02.723 --> 00:56:05.163
and professional and political life.

00:56:06.120 --> 00:56:08.370
The other one channeled
it in different ways.

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:14.363
So I think that's probably
what's explaining that variation.

00:56:15.940 --> 00:56:19.170
Second wait so this idea
of professional repression,

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:20.590
goes to your question too.

00:56:20.590 --> 00:56:25.533
So initially, they're not
directly repressing Mohamed Ali.

00:56:27.030 --> 00:56:28.160
They do shut down his account,

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:30.410
every time there's kind
of those vertical spikes,

